Goal
Fix the bug where REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE environment variable overrides an 
explicitly disabled session.verify=False, by ensuring merge_environment_settings
in sessions.py only applies CA bundle env vars when verify is truthy or None.

Files to modify
  • requests/sessions.py

Implementation steps
  1. Step 1: In requests/sessions.py, inside 
Session.merge_environment_settings(), locate the block that reads 
os.environ.get('REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE') and os.environ.get('CURL_CA_BUNDLE') and 
assigns them to verify.
  2. Step 2: Wrap or alter that block so it only executes when verify is not 
explicitly False. For example, change the condition from `if verify:` or an 
unconditional assignment to `if verify is not False:`, but ensure that when 
verify is True or None, the environment CA bundle variables are still read and 
applied.
  3. Step 3: Add or update unit tests in tests/test_requests.py (or an existing 
environment-settings test file) to cover the case where session.verify = False 
and REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE is set to an invalid path; verify that no OSError/SSL 
configuration error is raised.

Tests to run
  $ pytest tests/test_requests.py -k verify
  $ pytest tests/

Risks
  ⚠  Changing the condition might accidentally affect the default None case. 
Ensure that verify=None still reads the environment variable (preserving 
existing behavior for unset verify).
  ⚠  Verify no regression when verify is a string path and env vars are set; the
string path should still take precedence or behave as before.

